orbis-compat: a caller-set thread stack is kept (policy keep|small|floor)

The pthread_create interposer raised every request below its floor (the size of the
thread that first resolved it: 16 MiB in the ps4-wine title) and rebuilt the attr with
only the detach state and the guard size, so a caller that set its own stack address
(Wine's spawn_thread: the thread's 1 MiB kernel stack, direct memory Wine owns) lost it
and each thread cost a 16 MiB libkernel stack (~12 MiB of flexible memory, measured on
the console 2026-11-01, p5w).

orbis::threadCallerStackPolicy() chooses what such a caller gets:
  keep  (default) its attr goes to scePthreadCreate untouched, no trampoline; a refused
        create is counted and falls back to small
  small a fresh stack of 512 KiB (configurable), with the trampoline
  floor the old behaviour
orbis::threadCallerStacks() reports kept/small/refused and the policy;
orbis::threadStackFloorPeek() reads the floor without resolving it (under keep, Wine's
threads never resolve it, and a reader's threadStackFloor() would resolve it from the
reader's own stack).
Callers that set no stack address are unaffected.

Against orbis-compat 6922a7b6f4291a28edd239f1a4db50ed7aa00359. MIT, as the files it
changes. Applied by scripts/ps4/build.sh compat to a clone under build/src.

diff --git a/include/orbis_thread.h b/include/orbis_thread.h
index 0a29ad1..e0fb16f 100644
--- a/include/orbis_thread.h
+++ b/include/orbis_thread.h
@@ -107,6 +107,34 @@ void threadAltStacks(unsigned long *installed, unsigned long *failed, unsigned l
 /// The size of the alternate signal stack each thread gets, in bytes.
 size_t threadAltStackSize();
 
+/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ caller-set stacks (patch 0001)
+ *
+ * A caller that set a stack ADDRESS (pthread_attr_setstack) chose its stack and owns it. Raising it
+ * like a caller that chose nothing drops that stack: Wine's threads then each took a floor-sized
+ * stack (16 MiB, ~12 MiB of flexible memory) beside the 1 MiB kernel stack Wine had made for them.
+ *
+ *   keep  (default)  the caller's attr goes to scePthreadCreate untouched, no trampoline (the caller
+ *                    owns its signal stack too). A refused create falls back to small, counted.
+ *   small            a fresh stack of smallBytes (default 512 KiB) instead, with the trampoline.
+ *   floor            the caller is raised like any other (the behaviour before this patch).
+ *
+ * A caller that set no address is unaffected by the policy. */
+enum { kCallerStackKeep = 0, kCallerStackSmall = 1, kCallerStackFloor = 2 };
+
+/// Sets the policy for caller-set stacks; smallBytes below PTHREAD_STACK_MIN (0) keeps the current
+/// small size. An unknown policy is ignored.
+void threadCallerStackPolicy(int policy, size_t smallBytes);
+
+/// The floor if it has been resolved, else 0, without resolving it. threadStackFloor() resolves it
+/// from the CALLING thread's stack, so a reader that has created no thread through this copy would
+/// set the floor for every later thread (patch 0001: under keep, Wine's threads never resolve it).
+size_t threadStackFloorPeek();
+
+/// Threads created on the caller's own stack, given the small stack instead (small, or keep
+/// refused), how many keeps libkernel refused, and the policy in force.
+void threadCallerStacks(unsigned long *kept, unsigned long *small, unsigned long *refused,
+                        int *policy);
+
 }
 
 #endif /* _ORBIS_THREAD_H */
diff --git a/src/orbis_thread.cpp b/src/orbis_thread.cpp
index d609bd5..a9b08c2 100644
--- a/src/orbis_thread.cpp
+++ b/src/orbis_thread.cpp
@@ -32,6 +32,18 @@ size_t g_defaultStack = 0;
 std::atomic<unsigned long> g_created{0};
 std::atomic<unsigned long> g_raised{0};
 
+// Caller-stack policy (patch 0001): what a caller that set its own stack ADDRESS gets. Such a
+// caller (Wine's spawn_thread: the thread's 1 MiB kernel stack, in direct memory it owns) was raised
+// like any other, so its stack was dropped and every thread cost a floor-sized stack out of the
+// flexible pool: ~12 MiB each, measured on the console 2026-11-01 (p5w, 8 live threads 98304 KiB).
+// keep passes the caller's attr through untouched; small gives it a fresh stack of g_callerSmall
+// (also the fallback when libkernel refuses the caller's stack); floor is the old behaviour.
+std::atomic<int>           g_callerPolicy{orbis::kCallerStackKeep};
+std::atomic<size_t>        g_callerSmall{512*1024};
+std::atomic<unsigned long> g_kept{0};
+std::atomic<unsigned long> g_small{0};
+std::atomic<unsigned long> g_keepRefused{0};
+
 // ⚠ THE INTERPOSER USED TO LOG ONLY ON SUCCESS, AND THAT IS WHY THE ONE FAILURE THIS PORT KEEPS
 // MEETING HAS NEVER BEEN NAMED FROM OUR SIDE.
 //
@@ -317,6 +329,11 @@ size_t orbis::threadStackFloor() {
   return resolveFloor();
   }
 
+size_t orbis::threadStackFloorPeek() {
+  const size_t v = g_floor.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
+  return v==~size_t(0) ? 0 : v;
+  }
+
 void orbis::threadFailures(unsigned long* createFailed, unsigned long* attrFailed) {
   if(createFailed!=nullptr) *createFailed = g_createFailed.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
   if(attrFailed  !=nullptr) *attrFailed   = g_attrFailed.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
@@ -338,6 +355,21 @@ size_t orbis::threadAltStackSize() {
   return kAltStackSize;
   }
 
+void orbis::threadCallerStackPolicy(int policy, size_t smallBytes) {
+  if(policy==kCallerStackKeep || policy==kCallerStackSmall || policy==kCallerStackFloor)
+    g_callerPolicy.store(policy,std::memory_order_relaxed);
+  if(smallBytes>=size_t(PTHREAD_STACK_MIN))
+    g_callerSmall.store(smallBytes,std::memory_order_relaxed);
+  }
+
+void orbis::threadCallerStacks(unsigned long* kept, unsigned long* small, unsigned long* refused,
+                               int* policy) {
+  if(kept   !=nullptr) *kept    = g_kept.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
+  if(small  !=nullptr) *small   = g_small.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
+  if(refused!=nullptr) *refused = g_keepRefused.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
+  if(policy !=nullptr) *policy  = g_callerPolicy.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
+  }
+
 // ------------------------------------------------------------------ the interposer
 //
 // ⚠ THIS DEFINITION WINS OVER libkernel's, and that is a linker rule rather than a trick: a symbol
@@ -352,6 +384,30 @@ extern "C" int pthread_create(pthread_t* thread, const pthread_attr_t* attr,
                               void* (*start)(void*), void* arg) {
   g_created.fetch_add(1,std::memory_order_relaxed);
 
+  // Caller-stack policy (patch 0001). A caller that set a stack address owns that stack and its
+  // signal stack: under keep the attr goes to libkernel untouched and the thread starts on the
+  // caller's routine, without the trampoline (its 64 KiB array would sit at the top of the caller's
+  // stack, where Wine puts its syscall frame). scePthreadCreate is called directly so that a refusal
+  // is not counted as a pool failure; it is counted here and the request falls through to small.
+  void*     callerAddr  = nullptr;
+  size_t    callerSize  = 0;
+  const int policy      = g_callerPolicy.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
+  const bool callerStack = attr!=nullptr && pthread_attr_getstack(attr,&callerAddr,&callerSize)==0 &&
+                           callerAddr!=nullptr;
+  if(callerStack && policy==orbis::kCallerStackKeep) {
+    const int rcKeep = scePthreadCreate(thread,attr,start,arg,"orbis");
+    if(rcKeep==0) {
+      g_kept.fetch_add(1,std::memory_order_relaxed);
+      return 0;
+      }
+    const unsigned long n = g_keepRefused.fetch_add(1,std::memory_order_relaxed)+1;
+    if(powerOfTwo(n) && orbis_log_enabled()!=0)
+      orbis_log("thread caller stack REFUSED: scePthreadCreate returned %#x for stack %p size %llu "
+                "(%lu refusal(s)); falling back to a %llu KiB stack of its own",
+                (unsigned)rcKeep,callerAddr,(unsigned long long)callerSize,n,
+                (unsigned long long)(g_callerSmall.load(std::memory_order_relaxed)/1024));
+    }
+
   // ⚠ THE TRAMPOLINE IS INSTALLED FIRST AND FOR EVERY PATH BELOW, INCLUDING THE ONE THAT RAISES
   // NOTHING. The stack floor and the alternate signal stack are separate questions: a caller that
   // chose its own 8 MB stack wants no interference with the size and still wants an overflow on
@@ -372,7 +428,10 @@ extern "C" int pthread_create(pthread_t* thread, const pthread_attr_t* attr,
     entryArg = sa;
     }
 
-  const size_t floor = resolveFloor();
+  // Caller-stack policy (patch 0001): small, or keep refused. A fresh stack of g_callerSmall in place
+  // of the caller's, whatever the floor is.
+  const bool   toSmall = callerStack && policy!=orbis::kCallerStackFloor;
+  const size_t floor   = toSmall ? g_callerSmall.load(std::memory_order_relaxed) : resolveFloor();
   if(floor==0)
     return createThread(thread,attr,entry,entryArg,sa);
 
@@ -383,7 +442,7 @@ extern "C" int pthread_create(pthread_t* thread, const pthread_attr_t* attr,
   if(attr!=nullptr && pthread_attr_getstacksize(attr,&want)!=0)
     want = 0;
 
-  if(want>=floor)
+  if(want>=floor && !toSmall)
     return createThread(thread,attr,entry,entryArg,sa);
 
   // Raise it. The caller's attr is const and may be reused for other threads, so the change goes on
@@ -409,7 +468,7 @@ extern "C" int pthread_create(pthread_t* thread, const pthread_attr_t* attr,
   const int rc    = (rcSet==0) ? createThread(thread,&raised,entry,entryArg,sa)
                                : createThread(thread,attr,entry,entryArg,sa);
   if(rcSet==0 && rc==0)
-    g_raised.fetch_add(1,std::memory_order_relaxed);
+    (toSmall ? g_small : g_raised).fetch_add(1,std::memory_order_relaxed);
 
   pthread_attr_destroy(&raised);
 
